# 3. Competitive and incumbent-distribution strategy

The brand is competing against more than other Asian beauty brands. The relevant competitive set includes:

- Large local and multinational beauty companies;
- Pharmacies and specialty-beauty retailers;
- Department stores;
- Marketplaces;
- Independent beauty sellers;
- Professional makeup artists;
- Social-commerce sellers; and
- Legacy direct-selling or multilevel-marketing companies.

## Incumbent strengths to map by country

Research should document, rather than assume, the importance of:

- Adviser relationships;
- Catalog ordering cycles;
- Home demonstrations;
- Samples and trial sizes;
- Neighborhood distribution;
- Informal credit;
- Installment payment;
- Loyalty programs;
- Product availability outside major cities;
- Recruitment and representative incentives;
- Local beauty education; and
- Post-sale follow-up.

The response should vary by market.

### Mexico

Assess the role of adviser-based selling alongside pharmacies, department stores, marketplaces, and independent beauty retailers. The brand should counter relationship-based distribution with:

- WhatsApp consultations;
- Creator-led demonstrations;
- Sample kits;
- Referral rewards;
- Reliable delivery;
- Local payment options; and
- Follow-up after purchase.

### Colombia

Assess adviser networks, social selling, beauty professionals, local retailers, and regional marketplace behavior. The brand should replicate the useful service elements—advice, demonstrations, and repeat contact—through:

- Creator-hosted consultations;
- Live Q&A;
- City- and climate-specific recommendations;
- Referral codes;
- Sample-led acquisition; and
- Human customer support.

### Brazil

Map legacy direct-selling networks separately from strong local brands, pharmacies, beauty chains, marketplaces, and independent sellers. Competitive countermeasures should include:

- Local Portuguese advice;
- Product availability and fast fulfillment;
- Samples and discovery kits;
- Installment payments;
- Professional education;
- Inclusive shade proof;
- Sensory and performance demonstrations; and
- Strong loyalty and repeat-purchase programs.

The brand should not criticize MLM companies publicly. It should compete on transparency, product proof, convenience, and service. Any ambassador program must reward verified product sales or educational activity, not recruitment, inventory loading, or downline commissions.

