# 6. Logistics and inventory model

## Recommended operating model

For the first year, use **in-country importation plus a local 3PL**, with marketplace fulfillment added selectively.

### Why not rely primarily on cross-border shipping?

Cross-border D2C may reduce initial inventory risk, but it creates:

- Longer delivery times;
- Customs uncertainty;
- Duties and tax surprises;
- More difficult returns;
- Higher customer-service burden;
- Lower marketplace ranking;
- Greater risk of damaged or delayed products; and
- Less control over the customer promise.

Cross-border shipping may be used for limited testing before local importation, but should not be the steady-state model for hero products.

## Operating design by country

| Market | Initial model | Key control |
|---|---|---|
| Mexico | Local importer plus 3PL in or near Mexico City; marketplace fulfillment for proven SKUs | Maintain nationwide delivery coverage without overstocking slow regions |
| Colombia | Local importer or compliant commercial partner plus 3PL; focus first on major cities | Validate customs lead times and regional delivery reliability before national expansion |
| Brazil | Brazilian entity or qualified importer, local tax structure, in-country 3PL, marketplace fulfillment for top SKUs | Model state-level tax, regional freight, installment economics, and reverse logistics |

## Inventory commitment

Use a staged commitment:

- **Pilot:** 8–10 weeks of hero-SKU inventory;
- **Soft launch:** 12–16 weeks for proven heroes, 6–8 weeks for test SKUs;
- **Scale:** reorder only after demand, margin, and complaint data are validated.

Maintain country-level safety stock based on:

- Supplier lead time;
- Customs clearance variability;
- Minimum order quantities;
- Shelf life;
- Stockout cost;
- Forecast error; and
- Marketplace service-level requirements.

## Stockout and service thresholds

Pause or reduce paid media when:

- A hero SKU has less than four weeks of reliable inventory;
- Stockout probability exceeds 10%;
- On-time delivery falls below 95%;
- Cancellation or fulfillment failure exceeds 3%;
- Customer contacts about delivery exceed the agreed baseline; or
- A marketplace service score risks delisting.

## Reverse logistics and product safety

Define before launch:

- Return eligibility;
- Opened-product handling;
- Damaged-product disposal;
- Batch traceability;
- Quarantine procedures;
- Expired-product destruction;
- Refund timing;
- Marketplace versus owned-channel responsibility; and
- Recall authority.

Returns should not be restocked if product integrity, hygiene, or tamper evidence is uncertain.

---

# 7. Pricing and unit economics

## Positioning

The recommended initial position is **accessible premium or masstige**, not luxury:

- Above mass-market products where formulation, packaging, or education supports a premium;
- Below prestige imports that have high distribution and retail markups;
- Accessible enough to permit trial through minis, bundles, and entry products.

The position should be tested by category. A sunscreen, serum, lip product, and complexion product may require different price ladders.

## Price waterfall

Every SKU should be modeled from consumer price backward:

\[
\text{Net contribution}
=
\text{Consumer price}
-
\text{taxes}
-
\text{marketplace/payment fees}
-
\text{creator commission}
-
\text{shipping}
-
\text{fulfillment}
-
\text{landed product cost}
-
\text{returns}
-
\text{customer acquisition cost}
\]

Include:

- Import duties;
- Customs brokerage;
- Local VAT or indirect tax;
- Marketplace commission;
- Payment processing;
- Installment financing cost;
- Samples;
- Packaging;
- Refunds and exchanges;
- Affiliate commissions;
- 3PL fees; and
- Customer-service cost.

### Brazil-specific pricing

Build separate prices for:

- Owned site;
- Mercado Livre;
- Amazon;
- Shopee, if used;
- Promotional bundles; and
- Installment payment.

Installments can improve conversion but may materially reduce contribution margin. The model should establish:

- Maximum installment count;
- Minimum order value;
- Whether the brand or payment provider bears financing cost;
- Promotional periods;
- Marketplace price parity rules; and
- Minimum acceptable contribution after financing.

Avoid unexplained channel price differences. If marketplace fees require different pricing, explain shipping, bundle, or service differences transparently.

## Illustrative first-year financial model

The following is a planning example using a **US$1.5 million initial launch investment**, excluding product manufacturing already committed. It must be replaced with actual landed-cost and tax data.

| Item | Regional planning assumption |
|---|---:|
| Net first-year revenue target | US$3.0–3.5 million |
| Orders | 85,000–105,000 |
| Blended average order value | US$32–38 |
| Blended new-customer CAC | ≤ US$18 |
| First-order contribution margin after variable channel costs | ≥ 25% |
| 90-day repeat purchase | ≥ 20% |
| 180-day repeat purchase | ≥ 30% |
| Owned-channel share by month 12 | ≥ 35% of orders |
| Marketplace contribution after fees | ≥ 15% |
| Inventory turns | 3–5 annually |
| Stockout rate for hero SKUs | < 5% of selling days |
| Return/refund rate | < 8% overall, with complexion monitored separately |
| Paid-media payback | ≤ 120 days |
| Customer-service first response | < 12 business hours |
| On-time delivery | ≥ 95% |

### Illustrative revenue allocation

| Market | Share of first-year revenue target | Illustrative revenue |
|---|---:|---:|
| Mexico | 35–40% | US$1.05–1.40 million |
| Brazil | 40–45% | US$1.20–1.58 million |
| Colombia | 15–20% | US$0.45–0.70 million |

Brazil receives a larger revenue ambition because of market scale, but also requires the greatest investment and operational readiness. These are planning allocations, not market-size claims.

## Marketing allocation against the US$1.5 million example

| Workstream | Share | Illustrative amount |
|---|---:|---:|
| Product testing, sampling, and localization | 20% | US$300,000 |
| Creator fees, content, and affiliates | 25% | US$375,000 |
| Paid media and marketplace advertising | 20% | US$300,000 |
