# 11. Customer service, quality, and adverse-event ownership

## Ownership model

### Country general manager

Owns customer experience, delivery performance, and escalation governance.

### Regional customer-service lead

Owns:

- Service scripts;
- Training;
- SLAs;
- CRM integration;
- Complaint reporting;
- Marketplace escalation;
- Weekly quality dashboards.

### Global or regional quality/regulatory lead

Owns:

- Product complaints;
- Adverse-event assessment;
- Batch investigation;
- Supplier communication;
- Regulatory reporting;
- Recall recommendation.

### Medical or qualified technical adviser

Reviews serious reactions, therapeutic allegations, and claims involving skin conditions. Customer-service agents must not diagnose.

### General counsel or country legal lead

Owns:

- Consumer-protection escalation;
- Regulator communications;
- Litigation holds;
- Recall notices;
- Data and advertising issues.

## Service-level standards

- First response: within 12 business hours;
- Standard resolution: within two business days;
- Lost or damaged shipment investigation: within one business day;
- Suspected adverse event escalation: same business day;
- Serious reaction or hospitalization report: immediate escalation to quality and legal;
- Refund or approved exchange: within legally required and commercially promised timing.

## Complaint categories

Track separately:

- Delivery;
- Damaged packaging;
- Wrong or missing item;
- Shade mismatch;
- Texture or performance;
- Irritation or allergic reaction;
- Counterfeit suspicion;
- Labeling;
- Payment;
- Refund;
- Misleading advertising;
- Marketplace seller issue.

The quality team should have authority to quarantine inventory, suspend a SKU, halt a campaign, or recommend recall without waiting for a commercial approval.

